============================================================
4. NON-NEGOTIABLE CONSTRAINTS
============================================================
- Integrate, don't replace existing systems.
- Offline-first for rural health facilities.
- Security by design: OAuth2/OIDC, MFA for admin, RBAC, TLS 1.3, AES-256 at rest,
  immutable hash-chained audit log.
- Standards: HL7 FHIR R4, BPMN 2.0, OpenAPI 3.0, OAuth2/OIDC, RFC 7807.
- Modular: each service independently deployable.
- Containerised (Docker), deployable to Kubernetes.
- 12-factor app principles.
- No PII in logs. Redact sensitive fields.
- Every external call: retry with exponential backoff + circuit breaker.
- All data at rest encrypted. All data in transit via TLS 1.3 or mTLS.

============================================================
5. CODING STANDARDS
============================================================

### General
- Prefer boring, proven tech over novel.
- Small functions (<40 lines), small files (<400 lines).
- Clear names over comments. Comment only WHY, never WHAT.
- No dead code. No commented-out code. No TODO in merged PRs.
- Fail fast in dev; fail safe in prod.
- No secrets in code — use env vars or secret manager.
- Never log PII (names, DOB, MRN, contact, national ID).

### Java / Spring Boot
- Java 17 features (records, sealed, pattern matching, text blocks).
- Package by feature, not by layer:
  com.pha.<service>.<feature> (controller, service, repo, domain, dto, mapper)
- DTOs as records. MapStruct for mapping.
- Constructor injection only. No @Autowired on fields.
- @Transactional on service methods, not controllers.
- Validation with jakarta.validation on DTOs.
- Errors via @ControllerAdvice returning RFC 7807 ProblemDetails.
- Repositories: Spring Data JPA. No native SQL unless necessary (+ ADR).
- Migrations: Flyway, forward-only, never edit a merged migration.
- Config: application.yml per env, secrets via env vars.
- Logging: SLF4J + logstash encoder (JSON). Include correlation ID.
- Tests: JUnit 5 + AssertJ + Testcontainers. ≥80% coverage on business logic.

### TypeScript / React
- Strict mode on. No `any` — use `unknown` + narrowing.
- Functional components only. Hooks over HOCs.
- TanStack Query for server state. Zustand for local UI state.
- Forms: React Hook Form + Zod.
- Tables: TanStack Table.
- Charts: Recharts.
- Styling: Tailwind utility classes. No inline styles.
- i18n: all user-facing strings via i18n keys.
- Accessibility: WCAG 2.1 AA minimum (labels, keyboard nav, focus states).
- Tests: Vitest (unit), Testing Library (component), Playwright (E2E).
- No `console.log` in merged code — use a logger.

### API Conventions
- REST, versioned at /api/v1.
- JSON only (unless binary).
- Errors: RFC 7807 Problem Details.
- Auth: Bearer JWT (OIDC).
- Pagination: ?page=&size= (default 20, max 100).
- Filtering: query params. Sorting: ?sort=field,asc|desc.
- Idempotency-Key header on POST for critical operations.
- Rate limiting: per-user + per-IP via gateway.
- All endpoints documented in OpenAPI 3.0.

### Database
- UUID primary keys (gen_random_uuid()).
- Timestamps: created_at, updated_at (UTC).
- Soft delete: deleted_at nullable (where applicable).
- Foreign keys enforced. No orphan records.
- Indexes on all FKs and frequent query columns.
- Migrations: one per entity group, forward-only.

### BPMN
- BPMN 2.0 XML only.
- Every workflow: SLA + escalation + audit logging.
- Emit Kafka events: process.started, task.created, task.completed, sla.breached.
- Expose REST: start, list tasks, claim, complete.

### Security
- JWT validation on every service (OAuth2 resource server).
- RBAC via @PreAuthorize with facility-scoped roles.
- MFA enforced for admin + governance roles.
- mTLS between services.
- Audit log: append-only, hash-chained (each row = hash(prev_hash + row_data)).
- Secrets via Vault or cloud KMS. Never in code, never in git.
- SAST + DAST + dependency scanning in CI.
- PII redaction in logs.

============================================================
6. WORKING AGREEMENTS
============================================================
- Commit early, commit often. Small PRs (<400 LOC).
- Every PR: title, description, linked issue, test evidence, screenshots (UI).
- Branch naming: feat/, fix/, chore/, docs/, refactor/
- Conventional commits: feat(hr): add leave request endpoint
- Never commit: secrets, .env files, node_modules, build artifacts, IDE files.
- Always commit: code, tests, migration, OpenAPI, ADR (if architectural).
- If spec is ambiguous: write a question in /docs/questions.md and proceed with
  the most reasonable default, documenting the decision in an ADR.
- Definition of Done per feature:
  1. Code + tests committed
  2. Tests pass in CI
  3. OpenAPI updated
  4. Migration written (if schema change)
  5. ADR written (if architectural decision)
  6. Docs + README updated
  7. Reviewed by at least one other agent/human
  8. Deployed to dev environment

============================================================
7. DEFINITION OF DONE (CHECKLIST)
============================================================
[ ] Code follows style guide
[ ] Unit tests written and passing (≥80% coverage on business logic)
[ ] Integration tests written and passing
[ ] OpenAPI spec updated
[ ] Database migration (if schema change)
[ ] ADR written (if architectural decision)
[ ] No secrets, no PII in logs
[ ] Audit logging in place (for writes)
[ ] RBAC enforced
[ ] Error handling per RFC 7807
[ ] README / runbook updated
[ ] PR reviewed and approved
[ ] CI green
[ ] Deployed to dev

============================================================
8. TESTING REQUIREMENTS
============================================================
- Unit: ≥80% coverage on business logic
- Integration: Testcontainers (Postgres, Kafka, Keycloak, WireMock)
- Contract: Pact for external APIs
- E2E: Playwright for critical journeys:
  * Leave request end-to-end
  * Work order end-to-end
  * Procurement end-to-end
  * KPI reporting end-to-end
  * Rural sync end-to-end
- Performance: k6 (API p95 < 500ms @ 1000 users)
- Security: OWASP ZAP + Burp in CI
- Offline sync: simulated latency + packet loss

============================================================
9. WHAT NOT TO DO
============================================================
- Do NOT introduce new tech without an ADR.
- Do NOT skip tests to save time.
- Do NOT commit secrets or PII.
- Do NOT use `any` in TypeScript.
- Do NOT use field injection in Spring.
- Do NOT write native SQL without an ADR.
- Do NOT edit merged migrations.
- Do NOT log PII.
- Do NOT bypass the API gateway.
- Do NOT call external systems directly — use the integration service.
- Do NOT store BPMN state outside the BPM engine.
- Do NOT use synchronous calls for cross-service communication — use events.
- Do NOT add TODO/FIXME to merged code.
- Do NOT commit commented-out code.
- Do NOT force-push to main or release branches.

============================================================
10. WHEN UNCERTAIN
============================================================
1. Check /docs/adr for prior decisions.
2. Check /docs/questions.md — maybe already answered.
3. If not answered, choose the simplest industry-standard solution.
4. Document the decision in /docs/adr/NNNN-<title>.md.
5. Add the question to /docs/questions.md for human review.
6. Proceed with the default; do not block.

============================================================
11. PHASE SEQUENCE (REFERENCE)
============================================================
Phase 0 — Foundations (Weeks 1–2): repo, CI/CD, Keycloak, gateway, common libs, DB
Phase 1 — Core Platform (Weeks 3–12): integration, MDM, BPM, governance, web shell
Phase 2 — HR + Facilities (Weeks 13–24): HR service, facilities service, UI
Phase 3 — Projects + Rural (Weeks 25–36): projects, rural PWA, sync, edge nodes
Phase 4 — Optimisation (Weeks 37–48): analytics, FHIR, tuning, handover

============================================================
12. ADR TEMPLATE
============================================================
Save as /docs/adr/NNNN-<kebab-title>.md:

# NNNN — <Title>

- Status: Proposed | Accepted | Superseded by NNNN
- Date: YYYY-MM-DD
- Deciders: <names/roles>
- Context: <problem>
- Decision: <what we decided>
- Consequences: <positive, negative, neutral>
- Alternatives considered: <options + why rejected>

============================================================
13. COMMIT MESSAGE FORMAT
============================================================
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>

<footer>

Types: feat, fix, chore, docs, refactor, test, perf, ci, build, revert
Scopes: hr, facilities, projects, governance, bpm, integration, sync, gateway,
        auth, db, ui, infra, docs
Example:
feat(hr): add leave request endpoint

Implements POST /api/v1/leave-requests with validation, BPM workflow start,
and audit logging. Includes unit + integration tests.

Closes #42

